Title: External RDP client to drive Zotero add-on install without GUI
Created: 2026-09-11
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-11T14:54Z claude created

2026-09-11T15:07Z claude note real-world validation, live Zotero 10.0.2 (headless, --start-debugger-server 6000, PID from /opt/zotero7/zotero -P default, no GUI session existed at the time so nothing was disrupted). Needed a THIRD pref beyond the two named above: devtools.debugger.prompt-connection=false, or every connection hangs forever behind a server-side allow-prompt (devtools/shared/security/auth.js's Prompt authenticator) that headless mode can never answer -- caught empirically, not by reading source, and now folded into bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js and this client's own docstring. Two eval transcripts: python3 bench/zotero_rdp_client.py --host 127.0.0.1 --port 6000 --timeout 15 "JSON.stringify(1+1)" -> "2". Then, read-only, against the real profile: (async function(){ const { AddonManager } = ChromeUtils.importESModule('resource://gre/modules/AddonManager.sys.mjs'); const addon = await AddonManager.getAddonByID('sdt-pack-sitter@search-works-for-zotero.invalid'); return JSON.stringify({present:!!addon,id:addon&&addon.id,version:addon&&addon.version,isActive:addon&&addon.isActive}); })() -> grip preview's embedded value: {"present":true,"id":"sdt-pack-sitter@search-works-for-zotero.invalid","version":"0.3.37","isActive":true}. No install/replace/disable/enable was driven. prefs.js was diffed byte-identical to a pre-launch backup after Zotero quit (the only observed side effect of the SIGTERM-based quit, a bumped toolkit.startup.recent_crashes counter, was erased by that same restore). make check: 1339 passed, 15 skipped, exit 0.
--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0727 (the sitter's disappearance) is parked: four deliberate transition
arms (first install, replacement, pre-fix payload, disable/re-enable) across
two machines all SURVIVED, and the leading unexplored candidate is
intermittency under VOLUME — nobody has run dozens of install/replace/disable/
enable cycles back to back, because each cycle needs a human click through
Tools -> Add-ons -> gear icon -> Install Add-on From File. 0727's own log
(2026-09-08) already tried and rejected the two obvious shortcuts: a bare
`.xpi` dropped into `<profile>/extensions/<id>.xpi` never registers, not even
across a restart (confirmed dead end, do not retry), and `xdotool` cannot see
Zotero's window on this host (native Wayland client, not enumerable by an X11
tool) — so GUI scripting is not available either.

This ticket builds a standalone external tool that drives Zotero's real,
persistent add-on install path over Firefox's Remote Debugging Protocol (RDP),
from OUTSIDE the Zotero process, so that 0727's next experiment (many
install/replace/disable/enable cycles, unattended) becomes possible. It is a
tool, not a fix: it does not itself explain or close 0727's disappearance
mechanism, and this ticket's exit criteria stop at "the tool works and is
proven read-only", not at "0727 is explained". The multi-cycle experiment
itself is out of scope here — scheduling it is the maintainer's call once this
tool exists.

## Why the two obvious RDP paths are wrong, cited from source

Re-derived independently against the Zotero build actually installed on this
host (`/opt/zotero7`, application.ini reports Version=10.0.2,
BuildID=20260909184950 — extract `/opt/zotero7/omni.ja` and
`/opt/zotero7/app/omni.ja` with `unzip` to get the same sources) rather than
taken on faith from a prior session:

- **`--remote-debugging-port` (CDP / WebDriver BiDi) is the wrong protocol
  family entirely.** Its implementation lives under
  `chrome/remote/content/{cdp,webdriver-bidi}/` in the app omni.ja (Gecko
  toolkit) and is a distinct subsystem (`RemoteAgent.sys.mjs`) from the RDP
  server below; it targets web content/tabs, not chrome-privileged eval.
- **The RDP `AddonsActor` (`chrome/devtools/modules/devtools/server/actors/
  addon/addons.js`) is the wrong actor.** Read verbatim: it defines exactly
  two methods, `installTemporaryAddon(addonPath, openDevTools)` (line 28,
  hardwired to `AddonManager.installTemporaryAddon()`) and `uninstallAddon`
  (line 78) — nothing else. `installTemporaryAddon` is Firefox's "Load
  Temporary Add-on" mechanism (about:debugging): it does not persist across
  restart and does not exercise the `extensions.json` machinery a real
  install does, so it cannot reproduce or even approach 0727's phenomenon.

## The real install path, cited from source

`chrome/toolkit/content/mozapps/extensions/aboutaddonsCommon.js`,
`installAddonsFromFilePicker()` (lines 235-285 in the extracted source), is
what "Install Add-on From File" actually runs once a file is chosen:

```js
let installTelemetryInfo = { source: "about:addons", method: "install-from-file" };
let browser = getBrowserElement();
let install = await AddonManager.getInstallForFile(file, null, installTelemetryInfo);
AddonManager.installAddonFromAOMWithOptions(browser, document.documentURIObject, install,
  { preferUpdateOverInstall: PREFER_UPDATE_OVER_INSTALL_FOR_EXISTING_ADDON });
```

This matches telemetry actually observed in this profile
(`installTelemetryInfo.method: "install-from-file"`, noted in 0727's
2026-09-08 log). `installAddonFromAOMWithOptions` -> `AddonManagerInternal.
installAddonFromAOM` (`modules/AddonManager.sys.mjs:2575`) -> `setupPromptHandler`
(`:3318`) -> `startInstall` (`:2189` region). Traced independently in this
session:

- **`browser` is never dereferenced as a live DOM/XUL element on this path
  with `source: "local"`.** `_verifyThirdPartyInstall` (`:3280`) returns
  `Promise.resolve()` immediately when `source` is `"AMO"` or `"local"`
  (`:3283-3288`), before touching `browser`. In `setupPromptHandler`
  (`:3318-3421`), the three feature-gated branches that would dereference
  `browser` as a UI target (webextension permission prompt, SitePermission
  prompt, the custom `web-install-prompt`/`addon-install-confirmation` path)
  are each compiled to a literal `if (false)` in this build (verified at
  `:3338`, `:3351`, `:3367` — read directly, not inferred), so control always
  falls to the final `else { resolve(); }` at `:3409-3410`. `browser` is only
  ever threaded into `installNotifyObservers(...)` (`:2169-2187`) as an opaque
  `wrappedJSObject.browser` payload field passed to `Services.obs.
  notifyObservers`, never read back. **`null` is safe to pass as `browser`.**
- Net effect: on this Zotero build, driving `getInstallForFile` +
  `installAddonFromAOMWithOptions` with `source: "local"` installs with ZERO
  interactive prompts — matching what is actually observed (installs register
  within seconds of the GUI gesture, nothing to click through).

## The runtime-enable mechanism (no restart required) — the actual finding of this session

0727's own log named `--start-debugger-server` (a CLI flag, needs a restart)
as the only known way to open an RDP listener, and flagged that as
disruptive if Zotero is already running live. Re-deriving the flag's own
implementation shows the restart is not load-bearing:

`modules/DevToolsStartup.sys.mjs`, `handleDevToolsServerFlag()`
(lines 1083-1160) — this is ALL the CLI flag does:

```js
Services.prefs.getIntPref("devtools.debugger.remote-port");   // default 6000, greprefs.js:1520
// ... gated on _isRemoteDebuggingEnabled() (line 993), which requires BOTH:
//   devtools.debugger.remote-enabled  (default false, greprefs.js:1515)
//   devtools.chrome.enabled           (default TRUE already in Zotero's own
//                                      defaults/preferences/zotero.js:1518 —
//                                      only remote-enabled needs setting)
const { useDistinctSystemPrincipalLoader } = ChromeUtils.importESModule(
  "resource://devtools/shared/loader/DistinctSystemPrincipalLoader.sys.mjs", { global: "shared" });
const serverLoader = useDistinctSystemPrincipalLoader(requester);   // requester: any object, it's a Set key
const { DevToolsServer } = serverLoader.require("resource://devtools/server/devtools-server.js");
const { SocketListener } = serverLoader.require("resource://devtools/shared/security/socket.js");
DevToolsServer.init();
DevToolsServer.keepAlive = true;
DevToolsServer.registerAllActors();
DevToolsServer.allowChromeProcess = true;   // required by root.js's getProcess(), see below
new SocketListener(DevToolsServer, { portOrPath, webSocket }).open();
```

None of this is restart-only machinery — it is five ordinary chrome-privileged
statements. Zotero already ships a privileged eval console that needs no
plugin and no restart to reach once: **Tools -> Developer -> Run JavaScript**,
implemented in `chrome/content/zotero/runJS.js` — `run()` (lines 8-52) does
`var win = Zotero.getMainWindow(); result = win.eval(code);` (or
`await win.eval(...)` in async mode), i.e. it evaluates arbitrary code with
the full chrome privilege of the main browser window. Pasting the eight lines
above into that dialog (after `Services.prefs.setBoolPref("devtools.debugger.
remote-enabled", true)`, a runtime pref write, no restart) opens a real RDP
listener in an ALREADY-RUNNING Zotero, with no `--start-debugger-server`
flag and no restart. The exact snippet is `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js`
in this ticket's PR, documented for the author to paste in when Zotero is
already live.

**This session did not use that path for its own validation run**, because
GUI automation to paste into the Run JavaScript dialog is exactly the
capability 0727 already established is unavailable here (`xdotool` blindness).
Instead: at the time of this session, no Zotero process was running on this
host at all (`ps aux` showed none, no `.parentlock` on the profile) — there
was no live session to protect — so validation used the CLI-flag path
(`zotero --headless --start-debugger-server 6000`) directly. Prefs were added
to `user_pref` in the profile's `prefs.js` before launch and the file was
fully restored (byte-identical to a pre-launch backup) after Zotero quit. The
Run-JavaScript route is what a FUTURE session should use when Zotero is
already live; documented, not yet exercised against a live session, but its
JS shares the exact same authentication gate the CLI path hit (next
paragraph), so it needs the same third pref.

**A THIRD pref was required, found only by actually running this, not by
reading the flag's own code.** `--start-debugger-server`'s own gate
(`_isRemoteDebuggingEnabled`) says nothing about it. Every socket accept goes
through `devtools/shared/security/socket.js`'s per-connection `_authenticate()`,
which by default (`devtools.debugger.prompt-connection`, true by default,
`greprefs.js:1511`) shows a server-side "allow this connection?" prompt
(`devtools/shared/security/auth.js`, the `Prompt` authenticator) BEFORE the
hello packet is ever sent. With nothing to click, the first validation
attempt hung: the raw TCP connect succeeded but no hello arrived within a
15 s client timeout, and a raw `socket.recv()` confirmed zero bytes in 10 s.
Setting `devtools.debugger.prompt-connection` to `false` (also `auth.js`:
`authenticate()` short-circuits to `AuthenticationResult.ALLOW`) fixed it
immediately. `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js` and this client's own
docstring both carry this now; the CLI-flag path needed it added to
`prefs.js` by hand since the flag itself has no equivalent option.

## The wire protocol, cited from source (not guessed)

Re-derived from the same extracted `omni.ja`, since getting this wrong silently
produces a client that "connects" but can't attach to anything:

- Packet framing: `chrome/devtools/modules/devtools/shared/transport/packets.js`
  — `[decimal length]:[UTF-8 JSON payload]`, no other separator (see the
  class doc at line ~98 and `_outgoing = this.length + ":" + this._data` at
  line 213).
- `shared/protocol/Request.js` (`write()`, lines ~62-81) and
  `shared/protocol/Response.js` (`write()`, lines ~44-59): a spec's
  `request`/`response` template keys become wire field names VERBATIM — e.g.
  `getProcess: { request: { id: Arg(0, "number") }, response: { processDescriptor:
  RetVal("processDescriptor") } }` (`shared/specs/root.js`) means the wire
  request is `{to: "root", type: "getProcess", id: 0}` and the response is
  `{from: "root", processDescriptor: {...actor form...}}`. A bare top-level
  `RetVal(...)` (no wrapping key) spreads its fields directly into the packet
  instead of nesting them.
- Attach sequence: `root` actor's `getProcess(id: 0)` (`server/actors/root.js:503`,
  gated on `DevToolsServer.allowChromeProcess`, which is why the arm sequence
  above must set it) returns a `processDescriptor` actor; that actor's
  `getTarget` (`server/actors/descriptors/process.js:169`, spec at
  `shared/specs/descriptors/process.js`) returns `{process: {...}}` whose
  `consoleActor` field (added via `_createExtraActors()` in
  `server/actors/targets/window-global.js:730-733`, confirmed by the explicit
  `consoleActor: this._consoleActor.actorID` pattern in the sibling
  `content-process.js:174`) is the actor to eval against.
- Eval: the console actor's `evaluateJSAsync` (`server/actors/webconsole.js:772`,
  spec at `shared/specs/webconsole.js:149`) replies IMMEDIATELY with just
  `{resultID}` and delivers the actual result later as an UNSOLICITED packet
  `{from: consoleActor, type: "evaluationResult", resultID, ...}` (comment at
  webconsole.js:763-764). A client that treats the first reply as the answer
  will read `resultID` as the result.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0727-the-sitter-uninstalls-itself-update-url.erg` — the ticket this
  tool serves; its Action 4 named this gap
- `bench/zotero_rdp_client.py` (new, this ticket) — the client: packet framing,
  connect/attach/eval, CLI entry point
- `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js` (new, this ticket) — the snippet to
  paste into Run JavaScript when Zotero is already live (the no-restart path)
- `tests/test_zotero_rdp_client.py` (new, this ticket) — tests against a local
  mock RDP server
- `bench/sdt_sitter_install.py` — 0727's own three-state verifier
  (`read_addon_record`/`verify`); the eventual multi-cycle experiment driver
  will need to call something in this family after each install this tool
  performs, but building that driver is NOT this ticket's scope

## Actions

1. Implement `bench/zotero_rdp_client.py`: a packet-framing transport
   (`[len]:[json]`), a thin RDP connection (send/receive, dispatch unsolicited
   events separately from direct responses), and a `ZoteroRDPClient` with
   `connect()`, `get_chrome_console_actor()` (root -> getProcess(0) ->
   getTarget -> consoleActor) and `eval_js(code, timeout)` (evaluateJSAsync +
   wait for the matching `evaluationResult` event, raising on a JS exception
   result rather than returning it silently).
2. Implement `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js`: the documented snippet for
   Run JavaScript, cited above, for a session that finds Zotero already live.
3. Write `tests/test_zotero_rdp_client.py` against a small mock RDP server
   (plain `socket`/`threading`, no real Zotero, no subprocess needed for the
   server itself) covering: packet round-trip framing, the connect/hello
   packet, the getProcess/getTarget/eval happy path, a JS-exception result,
   a connection dropped mid-request, and a read timeout.
4. Validate the client against the real, live Zotero build on this host with
   READ-ONLY operations only (connect, attach, `1+1`, `AddonManager.
   getAddonByID(...)`) and record the actual output. Do NOT drive install,
   replace, disable, or enable through this client in this ticket.

## Test

Red step: `tests/test_zotero_rdp_client.py::test_eval_returns_result_not_ack`
— asserts that `eval_js` on a mock server that replies to `evaluateJSAsync`
with an immediate `{resultID}` ack, then later emits the unsolicited
`{type: "evaluationResult", resultID, result: ...}` event, returns the
EVENT's result rather than the ack. A client that reads the first reply is
the exact defect the wire-protocol note above exists to prevent, so this
test must fail against that naive implementation before the fix.

## Verification

- [x] `make check` is green with the new files in place (1339 passed, 15
      skipped, exit 0)
- [x] `tests/test_zotero_rdp_client.py` passes using only the local mock
      server — no real Zotero process, no network beyond localhost (10/10)
- [x] The client, run against the real live build (10.0.2, headless,
      `--start-debugger-server 6000` + the three prefs above), connects,
      attaches to the chrome/parent-process target, and returns a correct
      result for one inert eval and one read-only `AddonManager` query; see
      this ticket's log for the pasted output
- [x] No install/replace/disable/enable cycle was driven through this client
      against the real profile in this ticket — the only add-on-related call
      made was the read-only `AddonManager.getAddonByID` query above

## Invariants

- `make check` stays green with no dependency on a live Zotero instance
  (`bench/check_deps.py` must not gain a new required package for this; if
  the client needs one beyond the standard library, declare it in
  `requirements-drivers.txt`, not `requirements-check.txt`, and skip its
  tests gracefully — `@pytest.mark.integration`/`slow`-style — when the
  Zotero-dependent path isn't exercised by `make check`).
- Never call `installTemporaryAddon` or any AddonsActor method from this
  client — that is the wrong path this ticket documents, not an alternative.
- This ticket does not touch, and its exit does not depend on, 0727's actual
  disappearance mechanism.

## Exit criteria

The client exists, is tested against a mock RDP server with no live-Zotero
dependency in `make check`, and has been proven — by a pasted, real,
read-only eval transcript — to connect to and eval in a real Zotero's
chrome/parent-process target. The multi-cycle install/replace/disable/enable
experiment 0727 needs is NOT run here; scheduling it is a decision for the
author, flagged explicitly in the PR that closes this ticket.
